A||||16||Seanh||Dogbone Replacment: Poly or OE Rubber?||||5||
Z||000000||Seanh||10-30-2000||10:03 PM||||I took my dogbone out the other day and the bushings are shot. I was looking at the Fiero stock poly rebushing set and was thinking I should probally get it. I have heard that poly bushings transmit more vibration then the rubber ones, is that true? How much more is it then stock? And the obvious question: Should I get rubber or poly? If rubber, where can I get them? Thanks<P>------------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/singnit3_1.gif">   <BR>Sean Heberly<BR>Silver 86 GT 2.8<BR>Killer Stereo System||||reg||5
Z||000001||Bob Englert||10-30-2000||11:07 PM||||I have used poly dogbones in both V6 and 4cyl cars.  The poly does transmit more vibration which is VERY noticable on the 4 cyl cars.  I installed one on my Formula (now sold) and hardly noticed any difference at all.<P>You might notice more vibration if you have an auto transmission when idling in Drive.  I would recommend the poly if you are drivng a 4sp or 5sp.  <P>I think you can just get the bushings for the 86 dogbone.  Later years (or if yours has been replaced) used a conical shaped inner diamater that prevents using the poly replacement bushings unless you machine out the holes.  If this is the case you can get a Ultimate Dogbone from the Fiero Store.<P>I plan to use the ultimate dogbone on my 2 87 GT's I am working on this winter.<P>Bob<P>||||reg||1
Z||000002||fiero56||10-30-2000||11:12 PM|||| <A HREF="http://www.suspension.com" TARGET=_blank>http://www.suspension.com</A>  has poly buushings for dogbone with "rib" running outside of dogbone.<P>I have poly dogbone on my Formula 5-speed and there is no noticeable increase in vibration from engine. If the windows are closed, I can't hear or feel the engine running. Replace engine/tranny mounts while you're at it, never hurts.||||reg||1
Z||000003||fieroshop||10-31-2000||10:04 AM||||POLY DOGBONE NOT<BR>I have had to replace several dogbones that were the ultimate bone. These dogbones are hard and have been causing several problems.<BR>For instance, I had a customer (last one with this problem)who complained about a clunking noise when he accelerated and after more hten a hour we found the problem. His poly dogbone had elongated the bolt hole n the bracket which is bolted to the engine. The poly bushings in the bone are so hard that they will cause something to give. In this case the bracket. Results of all this<BR>two hours labor and a new dogbone. OEM<BR>Total cost to him was close to$225.00 after tax. Would have been much cheaper if he had not replaced the dogbone with a bone with poly bushings.<P>------------------<BR> <BR>      <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/fieroshop_sig_1.gif"> ||||reg||9
Z||000004||fiero56||10-31-2000||10:13 AM||||Possible the bolt hole was already elongated before the swap to poly? I think so. Poly probably just made the existing problem more noticeable. ||||reg||1
Z||000005||tgowens||10-31-2000||10:42 AM||||56, that's a possibility, but, like George said, we have replaced several. Let's put it this way, the purpose of the dogbone is to absorb shock from the torque of the engine -- the difference between a rubber bushing and a poly bushing is not just longer life with the poly bushing but less "give" -- kinda like the difference between a rubber tennis shoe heel and a leather dress shoe heel. <P>------------------<BR>              <IMG SRC="http://www.tgowens.com/tgowens_sig.gif">                          <BR><A HREF="http://www.tgowens.com/fiero" TARGET=_blank>www.tgowens.com/fiero</A> - Blue '87 GT - Gold '87 GT<BR><A HREF="http://www.cfog.org" TARGET=_blank>www.cfog.org</A> - Classic Fiero Owners Group||||reg||1
Z||000006||Bob Englert||10-31-2000||11:42 AM||||I kind of disagree a little with the above statement.  The dogbone is designed to prevent the torsional movement of the engine.  I don't see it as being primarily designed to absorb shock.  <P>I suspect the elongaged holes you repaired were due to the dogbone not being tight when installed, thus allowing the engine to slam the dogbone bolt into the mount hole.  The dogbone needs to be tightly secured to work properly.  <P>A tight dogbone will allow the torsional forces to go to the wheels where they belong!<P>------------------<BR>Bob<BR>2 - 87 GT's<BR>87 Coupe<BR>85 SE (For Sale)||||reg||1
Z||000007||theogre||10-31-2000||08:52 PM||||I'd use an OE strut on any stock setup BUT an 86.5 or later 4 cyl with automatic. Extensive notes about that later automatic are in my cave. <P>I don't like having the polly in my 87 4 cyl with auto but I like having broken exhaust parts and the TBI beat against the trunk even less. The vibration transmitted while idling in drive is considerable. I usually knock it into neutral at long lights.<P>Untill I can convert it to the old dual tranny mount setup the polly strut is a must to preserve engine parts. <P>Fieroshop & TG, What problems are you seeing? My polly strut has been on there over a year. Other than the very noticable vibration I've had no trouble with it or caused by it. In my case the OE ones caused me considerable damage none of which has recured since installing the polly.<P>I don't think polly is the panacia most people make it out to be but I'd like to have seen that bracket. ((In fact I think polly is way over rated in allot of applications.)) I doubt it was all the fault of the polly myself. likely some dink didn't check & tighten all the bolts before & after putting in the polly. I've seen brackets with missing bolts and even broken and all that was on them was the OE strut. <P>One fully OE setup I saw recently shook out one of the bolts that hold the braket on the block completely, shook a second loose and then broke the braket off the third. This was in a totally stock Olds with the DIS 4 cyl. The resulting engine motion broke the hose off the AC compressor and broke a fitting on the PS pump. Tore up a bunch of stuff enough to esentially total the car. All because no one bothered to ever look under the hood.<P><p>[This message has been edited by theogre (edited 10-31-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000008||Seanh||10-31-2000||09:21 PM||||I am thinking I will probally go with poly rebushing kit on the OE bone. I to and not a big fan of the ultimate dogbone from Fiero store. I have an 86 V6 Auto tranny. I am going to do a 3.4 conversion in February 2001. Since the stock heads go on the new engine, will they use the same brackets to connect to the dogbone? I just wanted to make sure so I dont spend money now for somthing that I will only need a couple months. Thanks<P>------------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/singnit3_1.gif">   <BR>Sean Heberly<BR>Silver 86 GT 2.8<BR>Killer Stereo System||||reg||1
Z||000009||Rednax Asbestos||11-01-2000||03:27 AM||||I've got the ultimate dogbone on my V6 and I like the exra vibration. It gives a feeling af extra power thru the seats.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> I estamate about an extra 10 HP. Thats emotional HP naturally.||||reg||12
Z||000010||tgowens||11-01-2000||03:42 AM||||<B>Bob Englert</B>, I can understand your disagreement but I guess I don't understand the difference in what I said and what you said. I said, "the purpose of the dogbone is to absorb shock from the torque of the engine" and you said, "The dogbone is designed to prevent the torsional movement of the engine". Actually, we are both right - the dogbone does both - the torsional movement of the engine is caused by the torque of the engine - no torque, no movement, right? No movement, no shock to absorb..........but, I didn't say it was primarily designed to absorb shock however, by absorbing shock, the torsional movement is reduced, right?<P><B>theogre</B>, the problems we are seeing have indeed been caused by loose strut mounts and/or loose bolts in the dogbone itself. I have found that if the lower strut is week it will allow greater force to be applied to the upper strut (dogbone) and with less resistance of the poly it has accelerated the wear in the mounting bracket itself. O am not faulting the poly itself but rather passing on the knowledge gained here at the Fiero Shop of the differences between the poly and rubber bushings. Certainly everyone has a preference and I just prefer the OEM part over the poly. My point was simply this - the poly will sertainly last longer but it is a harder substance thereby allowing greater vibration.<P>------------------<BR>               <IMG SRC="http://www.tgowens.com/tgowens_sig.gif">                           <BR><A HREF="http://www.tgowens.com/fiero" TARGET=_blank>www.tgowens.com/fiero</A> - Blue '87 GT - Gold '87 GT<BR><A HREF="http://www.cfog.org" TARGET=_blank>www.cfog.org</A> - Classic Fiero Owners Group<BR><A HREF="http://www.http://www.fieroshop.net/" TARGET=_blank>www.http://www.fieroshop.net/</A> - Fiero Shop - Richmond, CA - 1-510-23-FIERO||||reg||1
Z||000011||Haze_Performance||11-01-2000||06:44 AM||||My 2 cents....... NO POLY!!!<P>I used a Ultimate from the Fiero Store on my 85 & it was a huge mistake. The car shook so much at idle , I could have rented it out as a paint shaker. 2 weeks later I bought a new stock one. <P>Its a waste on a Auto car.<P><BR>Good luck  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/2d_00010aa3.gif">   <BR><B>Website's  <A HREF="http://www.WS6Fiero.com" TARGET=_blank>www.WS6Fiero.com</A>-                  <A HREF="http://www.hazeperformance.com" TARGET=_blank>www.HazePerformance.com </A></B>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/BURNOUT_4.gif"> ||||reg||4
Z||000012||fiero56||11-01-2000||07:32 AM||||Looks there are two "camps" on this debate Sean, take your pick.<P>Edited for spelling error.<p>[This message has been edited by fiero56 (edited 11-01-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000013||Master_Sushi||11-01-2000||10:59 AM||||I have an 88 coupe auto, and a ultimate dog bone. I DO get a lot more vibration with it. But it really tightend the whole engine up. Before if I would shift from {reverse, drive or park} in any combo. I could hear and feel the engine moving around. The air intake was also starting to slap the back of the trunk, keep in mind my car dosent have many miles either. So all in all I feel allot better for my engine and trannie with the poly. If I had a pre 86.5 auto I probably wouldnt have bothered getting poly though.||||reg||1
Z||000014||1FST2M6||11-01-2000||11:10 AM||||I have poly units. and like them... i had a solid bar there for a few drag runs and that'll break your teeth! MAN was that annoying.. but great for responce! <A HREF="http://www.cwestco.com" TARGET=_blank>www.cwestco.com</A>  has them in addition to the fierostore. <A HREF="http://www.leaderind.com" TARGET=_blank>www.leaderind.com</A> <P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/1fstsig1.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000015||theogre||11-01-2000||05:43 PM||||Wether you use the whole after market polly strut or retrofit polly to an old style bone is a matter of looks only. The end result is the same to the car's point of view.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000016||Seanh||11-01-2000||06:36 PM||||I see the poly kits everywhere, but where do they sell the OE rubber ones? OR do I have to buy the new dogbone complete? Thanks I dont know what Ill do, probally the cheapst. I will probally go poly for the 3.4, but the car is indeed an auto. I think I could live with a little more vibration, as long as it doesnt result in extra noise.<P>------------------<BR><A HREF="http://www.kdsi.net/~sean" TARGET=_blank> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/seanhsig.gif"> <BR>Sean's Fiero Page</A><BR>Sean Heberly<BR>Silver 86 GT 2.8 Auto<BR>Going on to 3.4 in Jan.||||reg||1
